<h2>A 1990s gold scheme, a 2016 award, a 2026 appeal</h2>
<p>The underlying arrangement dated to 1990: MMTC supplied gold and credit to Chauhan Jewellers for export manufacture, secured by a hypothecation agreement, an export agreement, a separate guarantee and the deposit of title deeds to a Delhi property. When the relationship soured, MMTC\'s claim of about Rs 57.74 lakh across five consignments — CJ-25, CJ-26, CJ-27, CJ-31 and CJ-1 — went to a three-member tribunal, which rejected every claim by its award of 26 April 2016 and ordered the title deeds returned. The single judge dismissed MMTC\'s Section 34 petition, and the matter reached the Division Bench under Section 37.</p>

<h2>Claim-by-claim review, not wholesale re-trial</h2>
<p>The Bench of Justices Anil Kshetarpal and Bharat Parashar opened with the governing discipline: a court does not sit in appeal over an arbitral award, and patent illegality under Section 34(2A) must go to the root of the matter — re-appreciation of evidence is not enough. What makes the judgment instructive is that it then applied that standard separately to each consignment, producing a split result.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old">
<h3>Rejections upheld</h3>
<p><strong>CJ-25 and CJ-26:</strong> the tribunal found MMTC had itself redirected the parcels when the original buyer failed to take delivery — a finding on conflicting evidence the court would not reappraise.</p>
<p><strong>CJ-31:</strong> the short realisation was attributed to MMTC\'s departure from the letter-of-credit mechanism and its handling of a dishonoured cheque — a permissible view.</p>
</div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new">
<h3>Rejections set aside</h3>
<p><strong>CJ-27:</strong> the claim was for interest on delayed payment, yet the tribunal answered it by inquiring into a shortfall, misreading cash-on-delivery terms as letter-of-credit terms. Patent illegality.</p>
<p><strong>CJ-1:</strong> the tribunal made collection of returned jewellery from Customs a precondition to a financing claim, contradicting the contract\'s express direct-recovery provisions. Patent illegality.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Because the CJ-27 and CJ-1 findings fell, the consequential direction to return the title deeds fell with them. The court was careful about what it did not decide: no amount was awarded to MMTC — liability, interest and quantum were left open — and no finding was made against the guarantor, who had not been a party to the arbitration. Precedents cited on the scheme\'s mechanics were held not to establish a single binding interpretation; the award was tested on its own reasons.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>Section 37 appeals rarely produce partial surgery of this kind. The decision demonstrates that patent illegality is not a slogan about the award as a whole but a scalpel applied to discrete findings: where the tribunal decided the wrong question (interest versus shortfall) or rewrote the bargain (adding a precondition the contract excluded), the court intervened; where the tribunal merely preferred one view of messy commercial evidence, it did not. Challengers should frame Section 34 petitions claim by claim with that distinction in mind, and award-holders should recognise that a favourable award survives best when each claim\'s reasoning answers the question the claim actually posed.</p>
</div>
<p>The appeal was accordingly allowed in part, nine years after it was filed — a timeline that is itself a quiet argument for precise, severable drafting at every stage of an arbitration challenge.</p>
